  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1203 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nWhile conditions are fairly mundane this afternoon, increasing \nclouds and chances for precipitation are expected overnight and \nthrough the day Saturday. As the tip of an upper level ridge \npropagates over our area, a return of moisture along with some 500mb \nvort maxes should be enough to spur the development of areas of \nscattered rain showers with a weak surface low pressure system. All \ntogether, the forcing is weak and ongoing convection to the south \ncould limit the amount of available moisture. Most rainfall amounts \nwill probably be 0.1-0.25\" or less. A narrow swath of instability \ncould spur some thunderstorm development but no severe storms are \nexpected. Precipitation activity will likely come in the form of on \nand off light to moderate rain showers beginning late tonight into \nearly Saturday morning and continuing through most of the day and \npossibly into the evening. Most precipitation should dissipate \novernight into Sunday. However, warm late summer water temperatures \non Lake Superior will be met with cool overnight temperatures \nsweeping in from the northwest Saturday night. This could lead to \nsome light lake effect rain along the South Shore Sunday morning \nbefore air temperatures warm up. \n\nBeyond the morning lake effect, Sunday should be a mostly dry day \nwith dropping dew points. That drier airmass sticks around through \nMonday midday before the next area of increased PWATs starts to push \nin from the Dakotas. That moisture should converge along a cold \nfront stemming from a shortwave disturbance to our north and sweep \nover the Northland late Monday into Tuesday. Pockets of instability \nmixed with 40-50 knots bulk shear may be able to develop some \nstronger to isolated severe storms, but time of day (overnight) \ncould limit the severe threat.  \n\nAs we remain on the north side of the ridge through the 7 day \nperiod, expect temperatures to stay largely right around normal with \nhigh temperatures in the 70s to mid 80s and overnight lows in the \n50s and 60s.",
